If you don't have access to Creo View SDK, Creo Toolkit, or any external CAD software, then unfortunately:

You cannot do true background rendering from a .PVZ file.

A PVZ file is a lightweight visualization package that contains tessellated/viewable data and is intended to be opened by Creo View-related software. It is not a format that Python libraries like OpenCV, Open3D, Trimesh, CadQuery, or Open Cascade can directly render.

Your realistic options are:
Option 1: Extract the PVZ and inspect contents
import zipfile

with zipfile.ZipFile("model.pvz") as z:
    z.extractall("pvz_contents")
    print(z.namelist())

PVZ is essentially a compressed package and may contain files such as .ol, .pvs, .ed, or .edz.

After extraction, check:

If you find .jt files → you may be able to use a JT reader.
If you find .obj or mesh files → you can render with Python.
If you only find .ol, .pvs, .edz → those are PTC-specific formats and are difficult to render without PTC software.
Option 2: Improve your current screenshot method

If you're stuck with PyAutoGUI:

Use Win32 APIs instead of full-screen screenshots.
Capture only the application window.
Use PrintWindow() from Windows API.
Avoid moving the mouse.

This can be 5–20× faster than PyAutoGUI screenshots.

Option 3: Find a hidden renderable file inside PVZ

Many PVZ packages contain internal visualization files. If one of those is a standard mesh format, you can render it headlessly with Python.

